# Homebrew No Ask

Homebrew 6.x makes ask mode the default for `brew install`, `reinstall` and `upgrade`. It prints `==> Do you want to proceed with the upgrade? [y/n]` and blocks non-interactive runs. That surfaces during nix-darwin `homebrew.onActivation` (`brew bundle` under sudo) and any other scripted brew path. The documented off switch is `HOMEBREW_NO_ASK=1`, per `man brew` under `-y/--no-ask/--yes`. It disables only the default; an explicit `brew upgrade --ask` still prompts.

**Why:** the prompt appears during an automated upgrade run, so the first suspect is the automation rather than a Homebrew default-behavior change. The fix is one environment variable, not a brew-bundle flag.

**How to apply:** Set `HOMEBREW_NO_ASK=1` to silence brew confirmation prompts under nix-darwin activation, CI, or any script. It belongs in the canonical shell env file, see [global-env-in-zshenv](mdc:.cursor/rules/global-env-in-zshenv.mdc), and is also set in `homebrew.onActivation.extraEnv` so root activation sees it. Learned 2026-06-22; topgrade wording retired 2026-08-08 when `build` became Nix-only.
